The first question to harshita was to create a CTE to find customers having rentals more than 30.

WITH rental_30 as (select * from rental group by customer_id, count(rental_id) having count(rental_id)>=30)
select * from customer where customer_id in rental_30;

I missed the second question because I was too busy sending my answer to professor on chat.
But the second question to Hritwik was again a CTE to find top 5 categories I guess but not sure.
I think the question was to find using CTE the top 5 categories which have been rented the most.
So the queetion was indeed to bring the top 5 categories based on the rental counts. 

WITH top5 as (select category_id, count(rental_id) 
from sakila.category
order by count(rental_id) DESC)

select DISTINCT category_id 
from sakila.category 
where category_id_in top5 LIMIT 5;


It was my turn when Amar sir asked me to solve the question:
find customers who rented atleast one with 'PG-13' film.
I did not expect three joins and the questions complexity to be that hard where in I had to introduce two bridge tables. 


MariaDB [sakila]> select c.customer_id  from customer c join rental r on c.customer_id=r.customer_id  join inventory i on i.inventory_id=r.inventory_id join film f on f.film_id=i.film_id where f.rating='PG';


This was the question I was asked and was able to solve and run the query sucessfully and get the output :)


MariaDB [sakila]> select c.customer_id  from customer c join rental r on c.customer_id=r.customer_id  join inventory i on i.inventory_id=r.inventory_id join film f on f.film_id=i.film_id where f.rating='PG';
 

